ver.\nUses `WaitForSingleObject`, unambiguous where `GetExitCodeProcess`'s\n`STILL_ACTIVE` (259) is not.\n\n- **Verified by:** script section 2. **Green on Windows in round 1.**\n\n### 4. No warning for a synced or network store — `internal/cli/doctor_synced_store.go`\n\nSQLite WAL corrupts on a OneDrive-synced or UNC directory, and `%USERPROFILE%`\nis commonly OneDrive-redirected. Doctor now warns.\n\n- **Round 1 found this one broken** — see fix 5 below.\n\n### 5. Atomic replace gave up on the first sharing violation — `internal/config/atomic_replace_windows.go`\n\nAdds the same retry loop its `brainstore` twin has. The duplication is forced by\nGo's internal-package rule and is now commented as such.\n\n- **NOT VERIFIED.** Nothing reproduces a `SHARING_VIOLATION`. The suite proves\n  it compiles and does not regress. Do not report it as verified.\n\n### 6. windows/arm64 shipped untested — `.github/workflows/test.yml`\n\nThe test matrix excluded a target `release.yml` and `nightly.yml` both ship.\n\n- **NOT VERIFIABLE here.** Only a CI run proves it.\n\n### Documentation — `docs/limits.md`\n\nRecords the 277-character export-path ceiling and narrows a lock-file security\nclaim that was true on Unix and only half true on Windows.\n\n- Round 1 **confirmed** the Go half on real hardware: 383 characters written and\n  read back with `LongPathsEnabled=0`.\n\n---\n\n## Round 2 — what the Windows run found, and what was done\n\n### 7. The store-path fix escaped the test sandbox (regression)\n\n`clitest.PinXDGRoots` is the `TestMain` of 25 packages and pinned only the four\nXDG variables. Once the data root read `%LOCALAPPDATA%`, every test resolving a\ndefault store on Windows walked out of the sandbox into the developer's real\nstore — and a plain `go test ./...` **created and populated one**.\n\nThis is the same mistake `testhome_test.go` was written to prevent, one level\ndown: redirect `HOME`, forget `USERPROFILE`, stay green on every POSIX shard.\n\n- `PinXDGRoots` 